### Niwa (Design system)

As the team continues to Zentraify all existing PureAccess assets, leadership desires to create designs that feel "native" to each platform that Zentra is running on. This means that the web platform will follow conventional web standards, iOS will be themed Zentra but follow common patterns of a native iOS app, and Android will do the same, potentially switching from the tab bar at the bottom of the screen of iOS to a hamburger menu. To accommodate these requests, I have restructured the design system into six libraries that build off of each other.

- Niwa | Core: Contains all brand assets such as colors, icons, illustrations, and a multitude of other design tokens that will be the same across mediums.
- Niwa | iOS: Extends the core library and creates assets that are specific to the iOS platform, such as the iOS typescale to accommodate accessibility, system colors, alerts, page-level back buttons, etc.
- Niwa | Android: Extends the core library and creates assets that are specific to the Android platform, such as the Android typescale to accommodate accessibility, system colors, alerts, fabs for primary actions, and a system back function.
- Niwa | Marketing: Extends the core library and handles all digital marketing requests and potentially creates a custom website outside of what agencies created for Zentra. Also creates email templates for properties and other resources that Zentra would use internally or to help properties during the transition to Zentra.
- Niwa | Print: Extends the core library and handles any print assets such as manuals or flyers for residents.


## Next steps

As mentioned above there are many steps that need to be taken to get the Zentra product to my standard. We need to continue the work on the design system and building out each platform a component/experience at a time. I am stressing to the team that we should focus and be very intentional with each piece of the interface and how it reacts to add a level of polish that customers feel trust in Zentra.

## Takeaways

As I continue to grow in my career I’m finding the hard part anymore isn’t the design work but the people. To make a world class design you need a team with great communication and prioritization skills. I believe with my experiences on the Zentra team thus far I am much more comfortable providing my opinion on how the team and product should operate to solve the customers need.

